---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Timers" description="How SIGRUN keeps time: calibrating the LAPIC timer against the PIT, a 10 ms tick, a hierarchical timer wheel, and the still-simulated HPET.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Subsystem 4 · timers</p>
  <h1>Timers: calibrating the LAPIC with the PIT</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Everything time-based — the scheduler's 10&nbsp;ms quantum, soft timers, the heartbeat — hangs off one
    interrupt. SIGRUN calibrates the CPU-local LAPIC timer by measuring it against the slow-but-precise PIT,
    then lets an atomic counter and a timer wheel do the bookkeeping in software.
  </p>

  <h2>Why calibrate at all</h2>
  <p>
    The LAPIC timer counts down at the CPU <em>bus frequency</em>, which QEMU and real hardware report
    differently. The PIT (8254) ticks at a fixed, well-known <strong>1,193,182 Hz</strong>. So the kernel asks
    the PIT to time ~10&nbsp;ms and watches how many LAPIC ticks elapse in that window — that ratio becomes
    the tick value programmed for the periodic timer.
  </p>

