  <h2>Timekeeping and the wheel</h2>
  <p>
    Each tick, <code class="mono">timer::on_tick()</code> runs three things in order: advance
    <code class="mono">CURRENT_TIME_NS</code> by 10&nbsp;ms (an atomic, so ISR and kernel code agree), fire any
    expired wheel callbacks, then hand the tick to the scheduler. Timeouts longer than one tick are the job of
    the <strong>timer wheel</strong> — soft timers bucketed by expiry, so checking "what expired?" is a couple
    of array reads, not a priority queue scan.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-scroll">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 180" role="img" aria-labelledby="wheel-title wheel-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%">
        <title id="wheel-title">Timer wheel layout</title>
        <desc id="wheel-desc">Four wheels of eight slots each with increasing granularity from 1 millisecond to 512 milliseconds, plus an overflow list for long timeouts.</desc>
