    <figcaption>
      Scheduling picks the finest wheel whose range covers the delay; expiry checks the current slot of each
      wheel. A 64-timer cap and a tiny overflow list keep it bounded.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>The tick pipeline, in code</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c">// timer/mod.rs — called from the LAPIC ISR after EOI</span>
pub fn on_tick() &#123;
    advance_time_10ms();                    <span class="c">// CURRENT_TIME_NS += 10 ms (atomic)</span>
    wheel::check_expired(current_time());   <span class="c">// fire soft timers whose time came</span>
    crate::scheduler::tick();               <span class="c">// preempt / run the next task</span>
&#125;
<span class="c">// lapic.rs — the measured ratio is the periodic countdown value</span>
let ticks = calibrate();
LOCAL_APIC.write_reg(Register::TimerDivide, DIVIDE_BY_16);
LOCAL_APIC.write_reg(Register::TimerInit, ticks);
LOCAL_APIC.write_reg(Register::TimerVector, (1 &lt;&lt; 17) | TIMER_VECTOR); <span class="c">// periodic · vector 32</span></code></pre>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">Why a wheel instead of a sorted list?</span>
      <code class="mono">check_expired</code> on a wheel is O(1) per slot checked — no heap, no reordering, and
      the whole structure is a fixed 4×8 array, so it works before any allocator complexity. The classic
      hierarchical-wheels trade-off is bucketing error: a timer lands in the first wheel whose range covers it,
      so it may fire up to that wheel's granularity late. 10&nbsp;ms ticks make that invisible here.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: timers</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why calibrate the LAPIC timer against the PIT instead of hardcoding 10&nbsp;ms?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          The LAPIC counts bus-clock ticks, and that frequency varies between real CPUs, hypervisors, and even
          CPU frequency-scaling states. The PIT's 1.193182&nbsp;MHz is a fixed architectural constant, so it's
          a trustworthy reference. The one-shot dance — arm the LAPIC at max, start a ~10&nbsp;ms PIT count,
          read how many LAPIC ticks elapsed — yields a per-machine ratio at boot. If the measurement looks
          impossible (0 or all-ones), a fixed fallback (625,000 ticks ≈ 1&nbsp;GHz ÷ 16) keeps the system
          booting on exotic hardware.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Why the ÷16 divider?" — The LAPIC initial-count register is 32 bits; without a divider the countdown would overflow the register at high bus speeds. ÷16 maps a 1–4&nbsp;GHz bus into a countable 62.5–250&nbsp;MHz range.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>What is the timer wheel for, and when would it misbehave?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Soft timers — timeouts, sleeps, retries — that must not block the scheduler. A wheel buckets a
          callback by its expiry's position in a short array instead of scanning a sorted queue; checking is
          O(1) per tick. It misbehaves (fails to fire or fires late) when a timeout crosses a wheel boundary
          wrong or exceeds the whole wheel's range — that's why an overflow list exists, and why the 64-entry
          cap makes <code class="mono">schedule</code> report <code class="mono">WheelFull</code> rather than
          silently losing timers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>The README says HPET is "software-simulated". What does that mean?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          The HPET driver declares the MMIO base (<code class="mono">0xFED00000</code>) and API
          (<code class="mono">read_counter</code>, <code class="mono">counter_period</code>) but
          <code class="mono">is_available()</code> returns false and <code class="mono">read_counter()</code>
          returns 0 — it's a named capability, not a working device driver. The system's clock source is the
          atomic 10&nbsp;ms counter, so nothing breaks; the HPET is the future replacement for "tenth of a
          millisecond resolution" once real hardware matters.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "When would you actually need the HPET?" — Sleep and poll intervals below 10 ms (e.g. network backoff) and better monotonic time for measuring IPC latency; the scheduler only needs the coarse 10 ms tick.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

